# temple -- generated by tools/cad/build_shells.py from tools/cad/shells/. DO NOT EDIT.
# the striped pavilion: podium, 12-column peristyle, striped 11x11 cell, open parapet
# 21.0 x 21.0 m footprint, 10.4 m tall. One piece per material group, all at the
# building's own origin: `tex NAME TILE` is per model file, so a wall and a roof cannot
# share one. The y on each line is the height that group was drawn at, put back: model.c
# rebases every OBJ's lowest vertex to zero, which would otherwise land the roof on the
# ground. Hand-placed extras, if any, follow at the bottom from
# tools/cad/shells/extras/temple.part
piece models/own/shell/temple_floor.obj   0 0.000 0   1 1 1   0 0 0   1.770 1.960 2.260  tex paving_stone 0.4
piece models/own/shell/temple_wall.obj   0 0.500 0   1 1 1   0 0 0   1.670 1.730 1.830  tex plaster_smooth 0.7
piece models/own/shell/temple_trim.obj   0 10.280 0   1 1 1   0 0 0   1.800 1.870 1.980  tex plaster_smooth 0.7
piece models/own/shell/temple_stripe.obj   0 1.400 0   1 1 1   0 0 0   0.230 0.570 1.290  tex plaster_smooth 0.7
piece models/own/shell/temple_gold.obj   0 0.500 0   1 1 1   0 0 0   1.000 1.000 1.000
piece models/own/shell/temple_dark.obj   0 9.500 0   1 1 1   0 0 0   1.000 1.000 1.000

# ---- from tools/cad/shells/extras/temple_facade.part ----
# temple's dressing: the kit pieces that are not part of the shell's own fabric.
#
# The ten painted bands and the peristyle's cornice used to be kit pieces (stripe_panel,
# cornice_run) laid over a plain box; both are now real geometry in temple.py (the "stripe" and
# "band" groups), so they are gone from here -- keeping them too would draw the same course
# twice and cost two more slots in src/props.c's 192-model cache for nothing.
#
# What's left is genuinely not fabric: the drum the dome used to sit on, four finials on the
# parapet corners, and the dome itself, knocked off the roof by the hurricane and lying on the
# ground in front of the porch. Coordinates are local to temple.part's own origin and yaw.

# the drum, standing in the open well at y 10.0 -- 5.6 across in a 10.4 opening, which is how you
# can tell something used to sit on it
piece models/own/arch/temple_drum.obj        0.000 10.000   0.000   1.000 1.000 1.000    0.0 0.0   0.0   1.55 1.60 1.68  tex plaster_smooth 0.7

# four gold finials on the parapet corners, a size down from the two big birds
piece models/own/arch/bird_finial.obj       -5.500 10.400  -5.500   1.000 1.000 1.000    0.0 0.0   0.0   1.00 0.90 0.70
piece models/own/arch/bird_finial.obj       -5.500 10.400   5.500   1.000 1.000 1.000    0.0 0.0   0.0   1.00 0.90 0.70
piece models/own/arch/bird_finial.obj        5.500 10.400  -5.500   1.000 1.000 1.000    0.0 0.0   0.0   1.00 0.90 0.70
piece models/own/arch/bird_finial.obj        5.500 10.400   5.500   1.000 1.000 1.000    0.0 0.0   0.0   1.00 0.90 0.70

# the dome, off the podium on the sand, tipped onto its shoulder with its gilt side to the path
# so you meet the outside of it rather than the hollow, resting on the podium's front step with
# its far side out over the drop. Twenty years of weather have taken the shine off, which is what
# the sheet-metal texture and the blue-leaning tint are doing to the gold underneath.
piece models/own/arch/temple_dome.obj       -6.500  2.100 -10.500   1.000 1.000 1.000   25.0 -70.0  0.0   0.88 0.90 1.02  tex corrugated 1.0
